O|
O|                         
T|                         FADE IN:
O|                         
O|                         
S|          EXT. UPPER MISSOURI RIVER/1820'S - EVENING
O|                         
N,G|          ANGLE ON A SINGLE COTTONWOOD LEAF... brown and crisp...
N|          clinging to its empty branch... the solitary sign of life on
N|          an otherwise barren tree.
O|                         
N|          A gust of wind... the leaf breaks free... flutters down,
N|          landing in the slow current of the Missouri. The last leaf
N|          of the fall, taking its final journey south.
O|                         
N|          As it floats along the surface, rising and falling with the
N|          current, all we can hear is the river's gentle movement...
N|          the trickle of water... the splash of timid rapids... until
N|          DISTANT VOICES invade this world... soft at first, but
N|          growing louder... LAUGHTER... SINGING.
O|                         
N|          And then our leaf CRASHES INTO A WOODEN BOARD... the BOW OF A
N|          BOAT. We hear the VOICES EVEN CLEARER... MEN'S VOICES, as we
N|          rise up the bow... see it's a FLATBOAT BEACHED ON A SANDBAR
N|          at the center of the river.
O|                         
|          Beyond the flatboat are the voices... TWENTY MEN of the Rocky
N|          Mountain Fur Company, making camp along the shore... hauling
N|          wood, building campfires, pitching squares of canvas for
N|          makeshift rooftops. And this camp is full of life because
N|          these are some of the first men to ever see this untouched
N|          wilderness... men with a whole new world just waiting for
N|          them to claim their share.
O|                         
O|                         
S|          EXT. CAMP - EVENING
O|                         
N,I|          CAPTAIN ANDREW HENRY, (late 20's), dressed in a buckskin
N|          jacket with long fringe... thick belt pulled tightly around
N|          his waist with two pistols and a knife hanging from it. He
N|          stands out among the others... like an imposter pretending to
U|          be a member of some exclusive club. He pulls off one of his
N|          gloves... examines the BLOOD-FILLED BLISTERS lining his palm.
O|                         
N,I|          From across the camp, JOHN FITZGERALD, (40's), solid and
N|          thick... dark eyes of a killer, watches Henry. He nudges
N,I|          MACE BOONE, (40's), a thief in a previous life.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           Likely got a splinter. Can't
U|           figure what to do without Mama here
U|           to pull it out for him.
O|                         
N|          Boone chuckles... spits in Henry's direction.
O|           2.
O|                         
C|                          BOONE
U|           Need a doc, Captain?
O|                         
N|          Henry looks up... sees Fitzgerald and Boone grinning at him.
N|          He slides his glove back on.
O|                         
C|                          HENRY
U|           Gather more wood.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald waits for Henry to turn, then gives his back an
N|          exaggerated salute.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
P|           (under his breath)
U|           Shame my Pap was a broken down
U|           drunk. Else he could've bought me
U|           a Captain's job too.
O|                         
N|          Boone snickers. Fitzgerald stomps his boot onto a branch,
N|          easily snaps it into two easy-to-carry pieces.
O|                         
C,E|                          FITZGERALD (CONT'D)
U|           We got a plan for these fires,
U|           Captain, or are we roastin' berries
U|           all the way up to Fort Union?
O|                         
C|                          HENRY
U|           Glass and the others will be back
U|           with some game, Fitzgerald. Just
U|           make sure you have the fires ready.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           My supper's in the hands of a
U|           hermit nigger, a kid and a dummy.
U|           Hell, my belly feels full already.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald's boot CRACKS another branch... and when it does,
N|          we hear the EXPLOSION OF A GUNSHOT.
O|                         
T|                          CUT TO:
O|                         
O|                         
S|          EXT. WILDERNESS - EVENING
O|                         
N|          And a CLOUD OF SMOKE surrounding the LONG BARREL OF AN
N|          ANSTADT RIFLE.
O|                         
N,G|          The smoke fades, and at the other end of the rifle we see the
N,I|          face of HUGH GLASS, (40's), African-American, one eye still
N|          closed, as the other calmly stares down that barrel.
O|                         
C,E|           VOICE (O.S.)
U|           Shit fire, you got him, Mr. Glass!
O|           3.
O|                         
N,I|          Glass lowers the rifle, as PIG GILMORE, (30's), fat and
N|          filthy, races past.
O|                         
C,E|                          PIG (CONT'D)
U|           Square as a barn door.
O|                         
N,I|          JIM BRIDGER, (17), young eyes that have already seen a lot,
N|          nods to Glass as he follows Pig.
O|                         
N|          Glass watches Pig and Bridger trot through the trees to a
N|          fallen ELK. Glass walks calmly after them, graceful in this
N|          world... his rifle so comfortable in his hand it's like he
N|          was born with it there. Pig crouches over the dead elk...
N|          grins up at Glass.
O|                         
C,E|                          PIG (CONT'D)
U|           Right where you said he'd be.
O|                         
C|                          BRIDGER
U|           But bigger than we figured. Gonna
U|           be a load to haul back to camp.
O|                         
C|                          PIG
U|           Have to split it up I reckon.
O|                         
C|                          GLASS
U|           Yep. Bridger'll take the tail, and
U|           Pig you haul the rest.
O|                         
N|          And Fitzgerald was right about Pig being a little slow,
N|          because he just nods... pulls out his knife to cut the tail
N|          off for Bridger.
O|                         
N|          Bridger and Glass exchange a smile... until Glass notices
N|          something... steps over the elk, his eyes locked on the
N|          ground beside it. He runs a finger over the dirt... touches
N|          a broken twig.
O|                         
C|                          BRIDGER
U|           Another one close by?
O|                         
N|          Glass doesn't answer... studies the track... feels the nearby
N|          brush... inhales the scent from his hand. Bridger and Pig
N|          watch him... exchange a confused glance. And then in a flash
N|          of movement, Glass is on his feet, racing away.
O|                         
C|                          PIG
U|           Hugh?
O|                         
N|          Glass just keeps running. Pig and Bridger chase after him.
O|                         
T|                          CUT TO:
O|           4.
O|                         
O|                         
S|          EXT. CAMP - EVENING
O|                         
N|          The men building fires... laughing... enjoying themselves.
N|          Boone on the outskirts, gathering branches.
O|                         
T|                          CUT TO:
O|                         
O|                         
N|          GLASS - TEARING THROUGH THE WOODS...
O|                         
N|          ...dodging trees... leaping over fallen logs... loading his
N|          Anstadt as he runs.
O|                         
O|                         
N|          BRIDGER AND PIG - CHASING AFTER GLASS...
O|                         
N|          ...but not as gracefully. Pig trips... slams to the
N|          ground... scrambles back to his feet to continue on.
O|                         
T|                          CUT TO:
O|                         
O|                         
N|          BOONE - CARRYING AN ARMLOAD OF WOOD INTO CAMP...
O|                         
N|          ...seeing other men playing cards. He stops along the edge
N|          of camp, drops the wood to the ground.
O|                         
C|                          BOONE
U|           No rule says I'm the only one
U|           that's gotta gather this shit.
O|                         
N|          The other men don't even notice. Then an ARROW WHIZZES
N|          THROUGH THE AIR from behind Boone... THWACK... it hits him in
N|          the back of the neck... erupts out the front of his throat.
O|                         
N|          Boone stands frozen... confused... reaches up and grabs the
N|          bloody arrow... finally drops to his knees. And that's when
N|          a TRAPPER looks up... sees Boone on his knees, holding that
N|          arrow, his mouth open like a dying fish.
O|                         
N|          AND BEYOND BOONE ARE FORTY ARIKARA WARRIORS CHARGING THROUGH
N|          THE TREES... FEATHERS RISING FROM THE MOHAWKS SPLITTING THEIR
N|          SHAVED HEADS... FACES PAINTED FOR BATTLE.
O|                         
C|                          TRAPPER
U|           `REE!
O|                         
N|          WHOOSH... AN ARROW SAILS INTO THE TRAPPER'S CHEST, sending
N|          him flying backward. The camp explodes into chaos... men
N|          YELLING... grabbing for weapons... stumbling over each other
N|          as they duck behind trees.
O|           5.
O|                         
O|                         
N|          HENRY - PULLING THE PISTOLS FROM HIS BELT...
O|                         
N|          ...taking aim at the attacking figures.
O|                         
N|          The Arikara pour into camp, arrows flying... knives and
N|          hatchets swinging. And this is a massacre... the Arikara
N|          wading through the trappers... stabbing... clubbing...
N|          scalping. This once peaceful world is filled with a sickening
N|          mix of war cries and screams of death.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald rises up from behind a log... aims his rifle...
N|          BOOM... takes down one of the warriors. He starts reloading
N|          as ANOTHER WARRIOR charges him... draws back his knife.
N|          Fitzgerald pours the powder, but knows he isn't going to make
N|          it in time... the warrior leaps toward him...
O|                         
N|          ...BOOM... it's like the warrior hits an invisible wall...
N|          flies back to the ground, very dead. Fitzgerald spins...
N|          sees Glass and his Anstadt right behind him.
O|                         
C|                          GLASS
U|           GET TO THE BOAT!
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald takes off... flips his rifle around, swings it
N|          like a club across a warrior's head... WHACK!
O|                         
C,E|                          GLASS (CONT'D)
P|                          (TO HENRY)
U|           THE BOAT, CAPTAIN!
O|                         
N|          Henry shoves a TRAPPER toward the water. An arrow drives
N|          into the trapper's leg... he goes down. Henry lifts the man
N|          to pull him into the river, but several more arrows bury in
N|          the man's back... he falls limp. Henry FIRES HIS PISTOLS,
N|          dropping the WARRIOR.
O|                         
N|          Bridger and Pig join Glass... splash into the river, SHOOTING
N|          back at the attacking Arikara. The water deepens, and they
N|          start swimming toward the flatboat.
O|                         
N|          ARROWS HISS into the water all around them.
O|                         
N|          Glass is still in the shallows... aims his pistol... BAM...
N|          shoots an oncoming warrior... spins after the other men...
N|          joins them as they near the flatboat.
O|                         
N|          A final TRAPPER charges down the shore behind them. SEVERAL
N|          WARRIORS pursue him.
O|                         
C|                          TRAPPER
U|           WAIT!
O|           6.
O|                         
N|          He aims his pistol over his shoulder as he runs... pulls the
N|          trigger... CLICK... pulls it again... CLICK. But he's too
N|          scared to stop his finger... CLICK... CLICK... CLICK...
N|          THUD... as a hatchet buries in his back. He crashes face
N|          first into the shallows.
O|                         
N,I|          The Arikara leader, (ELK'S TONGUE), animal bones braided into
N|          his mohawk, a NECKLACE OF HUMAN EARS around his neck,
N|          straddles the dying man. Elk's Tongue grabs the Trapper by
N|          the hair, and CUTS OFF HIS LEFT EAR, then holds it up to
N|          Glass and the others, as he SCREAMS HIS WAR CRY.
O|                         
N|          Glass and the men shove the flatboat off the sandbar... start
N|          scrambling aboard, as arrows dart past them... drive into the
N|          wooden boat.
O|                         
N|          Suddenly a WARRIOR EXPLODES FROM THE WATER... tackles Bridger
N|          as he climbs up the side of the flatboat... jerks him back
N|          into the river. Bridger flails in the water... watches the
N|          warrior raise his hatchet high to slam down... just as Glass
N|          SLAMS THE BUTT OF HIS ANSTADT into the back of the Warrior's
N|          head.
O|                         
N|          The Warrior collapses under the surface. Glass grabs
N|          Bridger's hand... drags him up onto the deck.
O|                         
C|                          BRIDGER
U|           Thank you.
O|                         
N|          But Glass is already gone... FIRING BACK AT THE ARIKARA, as
N|          the current carries them away.
O|                         
C,E|           VOICE (O.S.)
U|           HEY!
O|                         
N,I|          Pig looks over the side... sees a frantic WILLIAM ANDERSON
N|          gripped to the edge. Pig pulls him onto the boat.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald and Glass grab LONGPOLES... shove them against the
N|          river's bottom to pick up speed.
O|                         
N|          Henry stands on deck, watching as Elk's Tongue yanks a DYING
N|          TRAPPER's head back by his hair to peel away his scalp.
O|                         
N|          Henry drops his eyes... can't watch. The TRAPPER'S SCREAM
N|          ECHOES OVER HIM.
O|                         
O|                         
S|          EXT. MISSOURI RIVER/FLATBOAT - LATER
O|                         
N|          Quiet and dark... the battle long over. The flatboat floats
N|          with the gentle current. The NINE SURVIVING TRAPPERS are
N|          scattered around the deck...
O|           7.
N,I|          Glass digging an arrow out of WALLACE MURPHY'S SHOULDER...
N|          Fitzgerald poling on one side with Anderson on the other...
N,I|          STUBBY BILL VANCE and Pig standing patrol with their
N|          rifles... Bridger doctoring a badly WOUNDED TRAPPER... and
N|          Henry standing at the front of the flatboat, staring off
N|          blankly.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           What's the plan, Captain?
O|                         
N|          Henry's still lost in those screams.
O|                         
C,E|                          FITZGERALD (CONT'D)
U|           Captain! What the hell do we do
U|           now?
O|                         
N|          Henry snaps out of his stare... turns to the men... obviously
N|          doesn't have an answer.
O|                         
C|                          ANDERSON
U|           We're just floatin' farther from
U|           Fort Union.
O|                         
N|          Henry's eyes instinctively look to Glass.
O|                         
C|                          GLASS
U|           The Missouri's no good. Not if the
U|           `Ree's running it.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           So we just float the hell down to
U|           Mexico or wherever else this river
U|           takes us?
O|                         
C|                          GLASS
U|           We get ourselves safe outta range,
U|           then track another course up.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           Add weeks to the trip.
O|                         
C|                          BRIDGER
U|           Better that than endin' up scalped
U|           on the side of the river.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
P|           (shoving him away)
U|           Shut up, kid, you don't get no say
U|           in this.
O|                         
N|          Bridger starts to respond, but Fitzgerald's angry glare stops
N|          him.
O|           8.
O|                         
C,E|                          FITZGERALD (CONT'D)
P|           (back to Henry)
U|           And in case you hadn't noticed,
U|           Captain, we're twelve men short of
U|           what we were.
P|           (off the badly wounded
P|                          TRAPPER)
U|           Thirteen before long.
O|                         
C|                          HENRY
U|           I understand our situation, Mr.
U|           Fitzgerald. We do like Glass
U|           said... put some distance between
U|           us and the Arikara, then chart a
U|           course to Fort Union.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald MUMBLES UNDER HIS BREATH... rolls those dark eyes
N|          to Anderson. Glass gives Henry a nod.
O|                         
O|                         
S|          EXT. MISSOURI RIVER/FLATBOAT - NIGHT
O|                         
N,G|          ANGLE ON THE BADLY WOUNDED TRAPPER... NOW DEAD...
O|                         
N|          ...as hands push the body over the side of the boat. It
N|          splashes into the water... floats downstream.
O|                         
N,G|          We PULL BACK... see that the flatboat is beached along the
N|          bank of the river. Glass and Pig watch the body drift away.
O|                         
C|                          PIG
U|           Reckon it's better `an lettin' the
U|           `Ree find him... take his ears...
U|           slice what's left all to shit.
O|                         
N|          Glass nods... steps down off the boat to Henry and the
N|          others. They're gathered around a map spread out on the
N|          ground. Henry runs his finger along a THIN BLUE LINE.
O|                         
C|                          HENRY
U|           So we hike west to the Grand, then
U|           follow it up to Fort Union.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           On foot? You got any idea how long
U|           that's gonna take? It'll be winter
U|           before we get there.
O|                         
C|                          ANDERSON
U|           Unless we come across a post...
U|           trade for some horses.
O|           9.
O|                         
C|                          GLASS
U|           No posts that far out.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           So if we do this, we do every step
U|           with our own feet.
O|                         
C|                          HENRY
U|           We make camp here for the night,
U|           then load supplies and head out for
U|           the Grand at first light.
P|                          (BEAT)
U|           And gather extra blankets `cause
U|           there won't be any fires.
O|                         
N|          More GRUMBLING from Fitzgerald and Anderson, as they move
N|          toward the cover of trees. Bridger walks up beside Glass.
O|                         
C|                          BRIDGER
U|           Thank you... for what you done back
U|           there.
O|                         
C|                          GLASS
U|           You'd have done the same for me.
O|                         
N|          Bridger nods... he hopes so.
O|                         
O|                         
S|          EXT. WOODS - NIGHT
O|                         
N|          Pig on watch... sitting at the base of a tree, rifle across
N|          his lap, eyes wide, scanning the darkness. All clear. He
N|          peers back to camp... Fitzgerald, Anderson, Stubby Bill, and
N|          Murphy wrapped in blankets... passing a bottle of whiskey
N|          around. Bridger across from Fitzgerald, poking at the dirt
N|          with a stick. Henry is off to one side, still studying that
N|          map. Glass sits against a tree, cleaning his Anstadt rifle.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald watches Glass a beat, then...
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           You treat that Anstadt sweeter than
U|           any woman, Glass.
O|                         
C|                          STUBBY BILL
U|           Never seen a woman that could stop
U|           a `Ree from three hundred feet.
O|                         
C|                          ANDERSON
U|           I knew a particular big-breasted
U|           redhead in Boston that might come
U|           close.
O|           10.
O|                         
N|          The others manage a small laugh. Glass just keeps working on
N|          that rifle.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           Bring it over here for me to have a
U|           look.
O|                         
N|          Glass doesn't look up... wipes a rag down the barrel.
O|                         
C,E|                          FITZGERALD (CONT'D)
U|           Did you hear what I said, boy?
O|                         
N|          Still nothing from Glass. And Fitzgerald doesn't like
N|          that... the tension's growing.
O|                         
C|                          BRIDGER
P|                          (MUMBLING)
U|           You seen it before.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald turns that mean, drunken glare on Bridger.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           What'd you say, kid?
O|                         
N|          Bridger hesitates a beat... until the other men's eyes force
N|          a response.
O|                         
C|                          BRIDGER
P|                          (SOFTLY)
U|           I said you shoulda already got a
U|           look when he saved you with it
U|           earlier.
O|                         
N|          The camp falls still... nobody talks to Fitzgerald like
N|          that... especially when he's drunk. There isn't a sound
N|          except for Glass working methodically on his rifle...
O|                         
N|          ...until the SWISH OF BLADE OVER LEATHER, as Fitzgerald tears
N|          the blade from his belt... flings it into the dirt between
N|          Bridger's legs... AN INCH BELOW HIS CROTCH.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald stares at Bridger, daring him to react. But
N|          Bridger's too scared... doesn't, so Fitzgerald just smiles.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           You're lucky you ain't a man yet.
P|           (off the blade)
U|           Else that mighta nicked somethin'.
O|                         
N|          A few NERVOUS CHUCKLES from the others, hoping this is over.
N|          And it is, because Fitzgerald reaches over and pulls the
N|          knife away... cleans the dirty blade on Bridger's leg.
O|           11.
O|                         
C,E|                          FITZGERALD (CONT'D)
U|           And unlike you, I don't need savin'
U|           by him or nobody else. Remember
U|           that.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald stands... wobbles just a bit. He slides the knife
N|          back into his belt, then steadies his drunken gaze on Glass.
O|                         
N|          The others exchange nervous glances... uh-oh.
O|                         
N|          Fitzgerald walks to Glass... snaps his fingers for Glass'
N|          Anstadt.
O|                         
C,E|                          FITZGERALD (CONT'D)
U|           Lemme see what's so special `bout
U|           that shooter of yours.
O|                         
N|          Nothing from Glass, so Fitzgerald grabs the barrel, but Glass
N|          holds firm.
O|                         
C|                          GLASS
U|           Middle of workin' on it.
O|                         
C|                          FITZGERALD
U|           Well you can stop workin' on it,
U|           and let me have a look like I said.
O|                         
